= Automaton (computation) — Technical =

== Transition systems ==
A simple deterministic finite automaton can be represented as:

<pre>
M = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F)
</pre>

where Q is a finite set of states, Σ an alphabet, δ a transition function, q₀ the start state, and F the accepting states.

== Recognition ==
For input w:

<pre>
w ∈ L(M)
</pre>

iff the extended transition function sends q₀ to an accepting state after consuming w.

== Nondeterminism ==
An NFA can have multiple possible successor states.

For finite automata:

<pre>
DFA power = NFA power
</pre>

for language recognition, despite different machine descriptions.

== Hierarchies ==
Changing memory structure changes expressive power:

<pre>
finite control → regular languages
stack → context-free languages
unbounded tape → Turing-computable languages
</pre>

